#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319010 :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) & =4{e}^{2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}]} \\\\  & =4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) & =ma(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2)|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319012 :question/problem "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-8t$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-8t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =4 \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319014 :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=(2t-9){e}^{-2t}$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2t-9){e}^{-2t}] \\\\  & =(2t-9)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-2t})+{e}^{-2t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-9) \\\\  & =(2t-9)(-2{e}^{-2t})+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(-4t+18){e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(-4t+20){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4t+20){e}^{-2t} & =0 \\\\ -4t+20 & =0 \\\\ -4t & =-20 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=5\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319015 :question/problem "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=4{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t+24$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(4{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t+24) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-12t+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(12{t}^{2}-12t+12) \\\\  & =24t-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24t-12 & =0 \\\\ 24t & =12 \\\\ t & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319016 :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $$\n\\displaystyle s(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12\n$$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319144 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}2x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{x} & ⇒ & v={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\int {e}^{x}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x{e}^{x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1\\cdot {e}^{1}-0\\cdot {e}^{0})-2({e}^{1}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2e-2(e-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319211 :question/problem "![](images/2d/2da83e2ec620d3920c4378a77785a9ef2802dd1d06badcf4852be2263f68264e.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=6{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=6\\sqrt{2}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=6{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{(6{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(6{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{36{e}^{2\\theta }+36{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{72{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /8}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319224 :question/problem "![](images/12/121012fc5671ada263e83ec6d3a897d187ecb3a9d56c8e4331a3c0c608a92622.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319225 :question/problem "![](images/be/be9e314ed11882842bbf9a74deeb3a994596c8275a4e854ffc66327fa0008058.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\pi )^{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319229 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{3}-4(2)=8-8=0 \\\\ y & =2(2)^{2}+1=2(4)+1=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,9).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{3{t}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{4(2)}{3(2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3(4)-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,9)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-9 & =x \\\\ y & =x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319230 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)=3e \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3e,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,e} & =\\frac{1}{e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3e,1)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4e}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1=\\frac{1}{4e}(x-3e).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{1}{4e}(0-3e) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{1}{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319247 :question/problem "![](images/59/59f053b9a122018d78cd9c30f5a53cd67cd2ff645d02ccb6d311e6183e081102.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}+2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2t}{3{t}^{2}+4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3t+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319248 :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319330 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}3x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{2x}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{2x}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2x}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\cdot {e}^{4}-0\\cdot {e}^{0})-\\frac{3}{4}({e}^{4}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}({e}^{4}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9{e}^{4}+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319331 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 4x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{4}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0))+\\frac{4}{{\\pi }^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }(-1-0)+\\frac{4}{{\\pi }^{2}}(0-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319332 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 4x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{4}{3}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{4}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+\\frac{4}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}(2\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{4}{9}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}(2\\pi \\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)+\\frac{4}{9}(0-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319333 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}9x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=9x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=9 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 9x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}9x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0)+(-1-1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319334 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(-18x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-18x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=-18 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (-18x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-18x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\cdot (-18)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(-18x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}+6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 0)-2(-1-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319335 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=9x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=9 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x} & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 9x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =9x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x})\\cdot 9\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}9x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3x{e}^{-3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-{e}^{-3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-3(1\\cdot {e}^{-3}-0\\cdot {e}^{0})-({e}^{-3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3}-({e}^{-3}-1) \\\\  & =1-4{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319374 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+14t+8 & =0 \\\\ (3t+2)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-4) & =2(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{15}{2}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) & =2\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{6}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319375 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)=-1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319376 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,-2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) & =2 \\\\ x-1 & =-4 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(-3,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319377 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2}-9t,\\,y={t}^{6}+2{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319378 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-8t,\\,y=2{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t-8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-8 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =8 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(4) & =6{(4)}^{2}=96\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319379 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-5,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $t=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320836 :question/problem "![](images/33/33939aec3ac1315771d36714d15b590980197aabf3c169cdea1a1108baf6d0fa.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320837 :question/problem "![](images/da/da2526ff7031c9dbc704b59e60c65df14dcd0678427e02c386fc16a86e1fc89c.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{cos}^{2}⁡t+25{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t\\,{|}_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320838 :question/problem "![](images/5f/5fdcb137c459d5631db3dc3d02ff33ca0b791619694f1badbe7f3f2d0b19141d.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}(2t+1)^{3/2},\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}(2t+1)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(2t+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2t+1)^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(t)^{2}+{((2t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}|t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2}+2)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320841 :question/problem "![](images/19/19f2adedfeac6e4c4af07c3d3742203020e10ce841dd22f8b2e22c36ee2bf8e1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(${{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))}^{2}+{({e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{e}^{4t}{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}({(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}(4{cos}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}(4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320842 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b9c4ebc430e05ef205e3d8108f2e1649bbc51b1a6b54e8f47b48e7ddad1e9a6.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y=4{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{cos}^{3}⁡t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{sin}^{3}⁡t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+144{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}12|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320849 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4d2cf1820b792d804ab4d1b17ce1f36c0b63e4f406f8692d47f64a823b805f1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡t-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9+9-18(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t-t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\ 18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =36{sin}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{18-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-6(-1-1) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320851 :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf06561072376949c6df49aec94b45c57b1cd43fbb031ea7b157ea5dfbac877a.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{9}(6t+1)^{3/2},\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{9}(6t+1)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(6t+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =(6t+1)^{1/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{((6t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}+(3t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{6t+1+9{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{(3t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}|3t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+t)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320857 :question/problem "![](images/89/893efa56ec41ef2242a48831ad4f58b23edc791464ccc7dcb4dd104b87843360.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =1-{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(1-{t}^{2})^{2}+(2t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1-2{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(3+\\frac{27}{3})-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320858 :question/problem "![](images/a6/a61d1a169971b5e0af320372885cdcbae794d163ab767685ef5b688a083aaadb.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2+4t,\\,y=\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2},\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+4t)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3/2})=4{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{(4{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16+16t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+t)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}(1+t)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-1) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321110 :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0b1dc90950ba127e90fce400bc7ed1b6e8dd8e710ff880ab5949521b02a258a.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=4{e}^{-t},\\,y={t}^{2}+6t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4{e}^{-t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t+6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t+6}{(-4{e}^{-t})}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)+6}{(-4{e}^{-1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8e}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321111 :question/problem "![](images/18/1817c65aea37112d93f2935801a0e7dcd654706a72f729810272f48e95d39665.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /6}=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{(-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321112 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=2.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6t}{2{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{3t}{{e}^{2t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2}{{e}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{e}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321113 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=5t-3,\\,y={t}^{4}+2t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4{t}^{3}+2}{5}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}+2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321114 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,y=8t-3,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=4.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4}=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right)}=16\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4} & =16\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =16(2) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321115 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t},\\,y=2{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{3{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$"}]}
